          "derivation": "Avestan has a classic heteroclitic r/n-stem here: nominative-accusative zafar- beside the oblique/derivational stem zafan-, exactly the ancient type of Greek ὕδωρ / ὕδατος or Avestan asan-/ašar-. Bartholomae AiW 1657-58: 'zafar- n. \"Mund (, Maul)\", uzw. daēvischer Wesen' (Yt.11.2, Yt.11.6, Yt.19.50, N.62) with 'Vgl. zafan-; zafah- und hazafāna-. — np., mp. (Pü.) zafar; vgl. nhd. Kiefer, ai. jámbha- m. \"Gebiss, Rachen\"', and separately 'zafan- n. sva. zafar-. Kompp. — np. dahan'. The two stems show up side by side in the compounds θri-zafan-/θri-zafah- 'three-jawed' (of Aži Dahāka) and vī-zafana- 'tearing the jaws open'. Nourai's bare 'zaf-' is the shared stem abstracted from these, not an independently attested word — a normal way to write the root in a chart. Semantically the word is marked: in Avestan it is used of the mouths of daēvic beings, and Pahlavi keeps the same colouring (MacKenzie 'zafar … mouth, maw (daevic)'). Nourai's cited authorities all say this: Pokorny 'With ptr. Avestan zafar-, zafan- \"mouth, jaw\"', Klein p.833 'Avestic zafar-, \"mouth, gullet\"', Buck p.221.",
